    "segment_type": "opening",
    "turns": [
      {
        "speaker": "Host",
        "role": "host",
        "utterances": [
          {
            "text": "Welcome to Bleak House Unpacked, where we explore Dickens\u2019s masterpiece one thoughtful conversation at a time.",
            "sentence_type": "intro",
            "is_quote": false,
            "quote_mode": "none",
            "rate": 1.0,
            "pause_before_ms": 200,
            "pause_after_ms": 300,
            "emphasis_words": [
              "Bleak House"
            ],
            "passage_ref": ""
          },
          {
            "text": "I am your host, Claire Whitaker, and I\u2019ll be guiding our roundtable through the fog and the fire of this novel.",
            "sentence_type": "intro",
            "is_quote": false,
            "quote_mode": "none",
            "rate": 1.0,
            "pause_before_ms": 0,
            "pause_after_ms": 300,
            "emphasis_words": [
              "Claire Whitaker"
            ],
            "passage_ref": ""
          },
          {
            "text": "Joining me are three distinguished readers, each bringing a different lens to Dickens\u2019s world.",
            "sentence_type": "intro",
            "is_quote": false,
            "quote_mode": "none",
            "rate": 1.0,
            "pause_before_ms": 0,
            "pause_after_ms": 300,
            "emphasis_words": [],
            "passage_ref": ""
          },
          {
            "text": "Eleanor Hartley is a novelist and creative writing teacher, keen on the architecture of sentences and the decisions that shape them.",
            "sentence_type": "intro",
            "is_quote": false,
            "quote_mode": "none",
            "rate": 1.0,
            "pause_before_ms": 0,
            "pause_after_ms": 300,
            "emphasis_words": [
              "Eleanor Hartley"
            ],
            "passage_ref": ""
          },
          {
            "text": "James Blackstone is a legal and social historian who knows the statutes and archives that underpin the Chancery\u2019s shadow.",
            "sentence_type": "intro",
            "is_quote": false,
            "quote_mode": "none",
            "rate": 1.0,
            "pause_before_ms": 0,
            "pause_after_ms": 300,
            "emphasis_words": [
              "James Blackstone"
            ],
            "passage_ref": ""
          },
          {
            "text": "Caroline Woodcourt is a lifelong reader and critic, whose personal responses reveal how Dickens\u2019s empathy works across a lifetime.",
            "sentence_type": "intro",
            "is_quote": false,
            "quote_mode": "none",
            "rate": 1.0,
            "pause_before_ms": 0,
            "pause_after_ms": 300,
            "emphasis_words": [
              "Caroline Woodcourt"
            ],
            "passage_ref": ""
          },
          {
            "text": "Today we begin with the fog that cloaks the opening scenes, and the strange fire that consumes Mr. Krook.",
            "sentence_type": "transition",
            "is_quote": false,
            "quote_mode": "none",
            "rate": 1.0,
            "pause_before_ms": 0,
            "pause_after_ms": 500,
            "emphasis_words": [],
            "passage_ref": ""
          },
          {
            "text": "Our first question turns to the paradox of data and grief.",
            "sentence_type": "transition",
            "is_quote": false,
            "quote_mode": "none",
            "rate": 1.0,
            "pause_before_ms": 0,
            "pause_after_ms": 800,
            "emphasis_words": [],
            "passage_ref": ""
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "speaker": "Host",
        "role": "host",
        "utterances": [
          {
            "text": "James, you\u2019ve argued that Jenny\u2019s dead child already had a name in the Victorian state \u2013 a mortality statistic in the Registrar\u2011General\u2019s tables \u2013 yet she speaks of a shared motherhood that no parliamentary paper ever managed. What is the state doing with all that data that Jenny isn\u2019t, and what is Jenny doing that the state can\u2019t?",
            "sentence_type": "question",
            "is_quote": false,
            "quote_mode": "none",
            "rate": 1.0,
            "pause_before_ms": 200,
            "pause_after_ms": 500,
            "emphasis_words": [
              "James"
            ],
            "passage_ref": ""
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "speaker": "James Blackstone",
        "role": "social_historian",
        "utterances": [
          {
            "text": "To hear Jenny, we must first hear the words she repeats, because they carry the weight of a statistic turned into feeling.",
            "sentence_type": "analysis",
            "is_quote": false,
            "quote_mode": "none",
            "rate": 1.0,
            "pause_before_ms": 200,
            "pause_after_ms": 300,
            "emphasis_words": [],
            "passage_ref": ""
          },
          {
            "text": "She says,",
            "sentence_type": "quote_setup",
            "is_quote": false,
            "quote_mode": "setup",
            "rate": 0.98,
            "pause_before_ms": 0,
            "pause_after_ms": 150,
            "emphasis_words": [],
            "passage_ref": ""
          },
          {
            "text": "It's my dead child that makes me love this child so dear, and it's my dead child that makes her love it so dear too... we mean the same thing, if we knew how to say it, us two mothers does in our poor hearts!",
            "sentence_type": "quote_reading",
            "is_quote": true,
            "quote_mode": "reading",
            "rate": 0.93,
            "pause_before_ms": 150,
            "pause_after_ms": 400,
            "emphasis_words": [
              "dead child"
            ],
            "passage_ref": "c22:p87"
          },
          {
            "text": "In the official mortality tables, that child is reduced to a number, a column; the state can catalogue, but it cannot feel the reciprocal love that Jenny creates.",
            "sentence_type": "analysis",
            "is_quote": false,
            "quote_mode": "commentary",
            "rate": 1.0,
            "pause_before_ms": 0,
            "pause_after_ms": 500,
            "emphasis_words": [
              "state"
            ],
            "passage_ref": ""
          },
          {
            "text": "What the archives achieve is the bureaucratic certainty of counting; what Jenny does is to convert that certainty into a shared, unspoken bond that resists any ledger.",
            "sentence_type": "analysis",
            "is_quote": false,
            "quote_mode": "none",
            "rate": 1.0,
            "pause_before_ms": 0,
            "pause_after_ms": 800,
            "emphasis_words": [
              "bond"
            ],
            "passage_ref": ""
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "speaker": "Caroline Woodcourt",
        "role": "literary_critic",
        "utterances": [
          {
            "text": "I hear that same refrain when I first met Jenny, and it struck me then as a moment of raw, unmediated empathy.",
            "sentence_type": "analysis",
            "is_quote": false,
            "quote_mode": "none",
            "rate": 1.0,
            "pause_before_ms": 200,
            "pause_after_ms": 300,
            "emphasis_words": [],
            "passage_ref": ""
          },
          {
            "text": "She confesses,",
            "sentence_type": "quote_setup",
            "is_quote": false,
            "quote_mode": "setup",
            "rate": 0.98,
            "pause_before_ms": 0,
            "pause_after_ms": 150,
            "emphasis_words": [],
            "passage_ref": ""
          },
          {
            "text": "...us two mothers does in our poor hearts!",
            "sentence_type": "quote_reading",
            "is_quote": true,
            "quote_mode": "reading",
            "rate": 0.93,
            "pause_before_ms": 150,
            "pause_after_ms": 400,
            "emphasis_words": [
              "mothers"
            ],
            "passage_ref": "c22:p87"
          },
          {
            "text": "For me, that exclamation feels like a promise that the novel makes to its readers \u2013 that we too will be bound together by loss.",
            "sentence_type": "analysis",
            "is_quote": false,
            "quote_mode": "commentary",
            "rate": 1.0,
            "pause_before_ms": 0,
            "pause_after_ms": 500,
            "emphasis_words": [],
            "passage_ref": ""
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "speaker": "Host",
        "role": "host",
        "utterances": [
          {
            "text": "Eleanor, you noted the em\u2011dash in the Krook scene \u2013 \u2018here is\u2014is it the cinder\u2026\u2019 \u2013 as the sentence itself breaking down. Could you read that passage and show us what it feels like to watch a sentence fail?",
            "sentence_type": "question",
            "is_quote": false,
            "quote_mode": "none",
            "rate": 1.0,
            "pause_before_ms": 200,
            "pause_after_ms": 500,
            "emphasis_words": [
              "Eleanor"
            ],
            "passage_ref": ""
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "speaker": "Eleanor Hartley",
        "role": "close_reader",
        "utterances": [
          {
            "text": "Listen to the description,",
            "sentence_type": "quote_setup",
            "is_quote": false,
            "quote_mode": "setup",
            "rate": 0.98,
            "pause_before_ms": 200,
            "pause_after_ms": 150,
            "emphasis_words": [],
            "passage_ref": ""
          },
          {
            "text": "Here is a small burnt patch of flooring; here is the tinder from a little bundle of burnt paper, but not so light as usual, seeming to be steeped in something; and here is\u2014is it the cinder of a small charred and broken log of wood sprinkled with white ashes, or is it coal? Oh, horror, he IS here! And this from which we run away, striking out the light and overturning one another into the street, is all that represents him.",
            "sentence_type": "quote_reading",
            "is_quote": true,
            "quote_mode": "reading",
            "rate": 0.93,
            "pause_before_ms": 150,
            "pause_after_ms": 400,
            "emphasis_words": [
              "burnt",
              "horror"
            ],
            "passage_ref": "c32:p131"
          },
          {
            "text": "Notice how the dash interrupts the clause, forcing the reader to pause as the narrative itself smokes and collapses.",
            "sentence_type": "analysis",
            "is_quote": false,
            "quote_mode": "commentary",
            "rate": 1.02,
            "pause_before_ms": 0,
            "pause_after_ms": 500,
            "emphasis_words": [],
            "passage_ref": ""
          },
          {
            "text": "The syntax mirrors the literal combustion; the sentence loses its footing just as Krook loses his body.",
            "sentence_type": "analysis",
            "is_quote": false,
            "quote_mode": "none",
            "rate": 1.02,
            "pause_before_ms": 0,
            "pause_after_ms": 300,
            "emphasis_words": [],
            "passage_ref": ""
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "speaker": "James Blackstone",
        "role": "social_historian",
        "utterances": [
          {
            "text": "That capitalized IS caught my eye, because Dickens often uses such emphasis to signal a legal rupture.",
            "sentence_type": "analysis",
            "is_quote": false,
            "quote_mode": "none",
            "rate": 1.0,
            "pause_before_ms": 200,
            "pause_after_ms": 300,
            "emphasis_words": [],
            "passage_ref": ""
          },
          {
            "text": "He writes,",
            "sentence_type": "quote_setup",
            "is_quote": false,
            "quote_mode": "setup",
            "rate": 0.98,
            "pause_before_ms": 0,
            "pause_after_ms": 150,
            "emphasis_words": [],
            "passage_ref": ""
          },
          {
            "text": "Oh, horror, he IS here!",
            "sentence_type": "quote_reading",
            "is_quote": true,
            "quote_mode": "reading",
            "rate": 0.93,
            "pause_before_ms": 150,
            "pause_after_ms": 400,
            "emphasis_words": [
              "IS"
            ],
            "passage_ref": "c32:p131"
          },
          {
            "text": "In the context of Chancery, the bold IS reads like a proclamation that the law itself is present, a living entity that can corrupt and combust.",
            "sentence_type": "analysis",
            "is_quote": false,
            "quote_mode": "commentary",
            "rate": 1.0,
            "pause_before_ms": 0,
            "pause_after_ms": 500,
            "emphasis_words": [],
            "passage_ref": ""
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "speaker": "Host",
        "role": "host",
        "utterances": [
          {
            "text": "Caroline, you\u2019ve said the capitalized IS still delivers a bodily jolt each time you hear it. If James reads it as a legal allegory and Eleanor sees the sentence combusting, what, in your view, truly accounts for that jolt?",
            "sentence_type": "question",
            "is_quote": false,
            "quote_mode": "none",
            "rate": 1.0,
            "pause_before_ms": 200,
            "pause_after_ms": 500,
            "emphasis_words": [
              "Caroline"
            ],
            "passage_ref": ""
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "speaker": "Caroline Woodcourt",
        "role": "literary_critic",
        "utterances": [
          {
            "text": "I think the jolt comes from the way Dickens forces the reader to confront the impossible \u2013 a man reduced to ash, yet declared present.",
            "sentence_type": "analysis",
            "is_quote": false,
            "quote_mode": "none",
            "rate": 1.0,
            "pause_before_ms": 200,
            "pause_after_ms": 300,
            "emphasis_words": [],
            "passage_ref": ""
          },
          {
            "text": "When the text shouts,",
            "sentence_type": "quote_setup",
            "is_quote": false,
            "quote_mode": "setup",
            "rate": 0.98,
            "pause_before_ms": 0,
            "pause_after_ms": 150,
            "emphasis_words": [],
            "passage_ref": ""
          },
          {
            "text": "Oh, horror, he IS here!",
            "sentence_type": "quote_reading",
            "is_quote": true,
            "quote_mode": "reading",
            "rate": 0.93,
            "pause_before_ms": 150,
            "pause_after_ms": 400,
            "emphasis_words": [
              "IS"
            ],
            "passage_ref": "c32:p131"
          },
          {
            "text": "That shock is less about legal symbolism and more about the visceral surprise of a word that refuses to dissolve, a reminder that the story\u2019s horror is alive within us.",
            "sentence_type": "analysis",
            "is_quote": false,
            "quote_mode": "commentary",
            "rate": 1.0,
            "pause_before_ms": 0,
            "pause_after_ms": 500,
            "emphasis_words": [
              "shock"
            ],
            "passage_ref": ""
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "speaker": "Host",
        "role": "host",
        "utterances": [
          {
            "text": "We have seen how data, syntax, and political metaphor each illuminate the fog around Bleak House.",
            "sentence_type": "transition",
            "is_quote": false,
            "quote_mode": "none",
            "rate": 1.0,
            "pause_before_ms": 200,
            "pause_after_ms": 300,
            "emphasis_words": [],
            "passage_ref": ""
          },
          {
            "text": "Next we will step into the architecture of injustice, exploring how Chancery\u2019s endless litigation shapes the lives of Richard and Ada.",
            "sentence_type": "transition",
            "is_quote": false,
            "quote_mode": "none",
            "rate": 1.0,
            "pause_before_ms": 0,
            "pause_after_ms": 1200,
            "emphasis_words": [],
            "passage_ref": ""
          }
        ]
      }
